<section class="band" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
    <div class="wrap split">
        <div>
            <h2 id="why-heading">Why one right answer isn't enough</h2>
            <p>A single correct answer can be recalled, guessed or pasted. Gadfly asks the questions a good examiner asks in person.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <p>It starts with "what is it?", then asks the student to explain their answer, defend it against a case that
                doesn't fit, and use it somewhere new. That is much harder to do without understanding the idea.</p>
            <p>Each follow-up is written from the previous answer, so it can't be looked up or prepared in advance.
                Understanding shows in the reasoning, not in the wording, and an unclear answer gets a neutral clarifying
                question that never hints at the answer.</p>
            <p>Instructors use their own course material. Gadfly turns it into learning objectives and rubrics they can edit,
                and every question and judgment cites the passage it rests on.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="path-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2 id="path-heading">From the first question to a released grade</h2>
        <p>A person stays accountable for every result. Nothing reaches a student until someone with authority approves it.</p>
        <ol class="steps">
            <li><h3>The instructor uploads course material</h3>
                <p>Text, Markdown, PDF, Word, EPUB or HTML. Gadfly drafts the concepts and rubrics; the instructor corrects and approves them.</p></li>
            <li><h3>The student answers out loud, on camera</h3>
                <p>A talking examiner asks each question with captions, and the student checks a live transcript of what they said.</p></li>
            <li><h3>A panel of AI models scores each objective</h3>
                <p>Models from different families score against the rubric and the cited material. Their grade is preliminary.</p></li>
            <li><h3>An examiner grades independently</h3>
                <p>Blind by default, without seeing the AI's view. Large disagreements go to a second reviewer to reconcile.</p></li>
            <li><h3>An authorized person approves the result</h3>
                <p>Only then is it published to the student, with appeals and amendments kept on the record.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="wont-heading">
    <div class="wrap split">
        <div>
            <h2 id="wont-heading">What Gadfly will not do</h2>
            <p>The research on proctoring and AI grading sets clear limits. Gadfly is built inside them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sheet">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Decide that a student cheated.</strong>
                    <span>Signals such as a face out of view or a tab switch are low-confidence notes for a reviewer. They are never a verdict and never part of the grade.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Store or send video.</strong>
                    <span>Camera checks run in the student's browser. Video frames never leave the device; only short descriptions of events are sent.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Mark anyone down for accent or fluency.</strong>
                    <span>Judges are told to ignore transcription slips, filler words and false starts. Pauses and speech rate never enter the grade.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Run an AI-text detector.</strong>
                    <span>Those tools are unreliable and flag non-native writers. Answer style is context for the reviewer only.</span></li>
                <li><strong>Claim more than it has measured.</strong>
                    <span>Gadfly makes no validity claims until its own pilot measures them.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

